Yes. And I think Orchid is better than my accidental Orchard. An orchard optimizes repeated known yield. An orchid represents something harder: a valuable emergence whose requirements may be subtle, relational, symbiotic, and initially poorly understood.

So I’d mature the existing Garden by making Orchid a subsystem inside it, then deliberately move the lens around the whole ecology.

Garden → Orchid

The Garden asks:

What conditions make useful things grow?

The Orchid asks:

What rare or difficult thing could grow here if we discovered its hidden requirements?

That distinction matters. Some ideas are weeds. Some are crops. Some are trees. Some are orchids: you can’t brute-force them by simply adding more fertilizer.

An orchid may require the correct combination of moisture, temperature, light, substrate, timing, fungi, pollinator, disturbance, competition, etc. Abstracted:

E=
f(S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_n,\;R_{ij},\;t)

The breakthrough may live not in any ingredient but in a relationship between ingredients.

So Orchid becomes the Garden’s machinery for conditional emergence.

