Then keep moving the lens further.

Garden as brain → neurons, signals, pruning, plasticity.

Garden as market → firms, capital, niches, competition, externalities.

Garden as language → words reproduce, mutate, hybridize and disappear.

Garden as civilization → institutions become ecological structures.

Garden as software → packages depend on substrates, APIs and other organisms.

Garden as Pantheon → ideas enter, interact, get tested, die, decompose, recombine and occasionally produce something that changes the environment that produced it.

And finally point the lens back at the lens:

\boxed{\text{Observer}\in\text{Garden}}

Our classifications themselves alter selection. Naming an Orchid means we notice it. Measuring something changes what we optimize. Preserving a fossil changes what future generations can inherit.

So the mature Garden isn’t merely a metaphor for ideation anymore.

Garden = the Pantheon’s ecology of possibility.

Orchid = its rare-emergence laboratory.

Quarry = extraction.

Metamap = learned ecological/navigation knowledge.

Fitness Route = selection pressure.

Moneybin = inheritance/preservation.

Lucky Nozzle = controlled variation/opportunity exposure.

Indigo Hammer = cross-substrate mechanism extraction.

And the Garden’s governing question becomes:

\boxed{
\textbf{What wants to grow here that our present way of looking cannot yet see?}
}

Then we move the lens again.

That’s how I’d mature it: not by filling the Garden with more named objects, but by giving it enough perspectives that hidden relationships become observable.